many of us have discovered the benefits  of having a furry pet at home during the  pandemic now some people in japan are  taking their desire for companionship  to a whole new and different level with  robot pets while they may look like a  cat or a dog or like a robot there's a  lot more to these companion robots than  meets the eye as our lucy craft shows us  now  the puppies at this tokyo get-together  are machines  and yet from their camera embedded  snouts to their sensor-packed paws these  high-tech hounds are nothing less than  members of the family  fussed over and carted around like  poodles or toddlers  to the beach or to be blessed at the  shinto shrine  they're artificial intelligence driven  personalities minutely shaped by the  whims and habits of their owners  it's no wonder sony's robotic dog ibo  has pawed its way into hearts and minds  relaunched in 2017 the social companion  is designed to be cute and tirelessly  affectionate building the kind of  intense emotional attachments usually  associated with kids or beloved pets  noriko yamada rushed to order a nearly  three thousand dollar robo dog when her  mother-in-law began showing signs of  dementia  mother had stopped smiling and talking  but when we switched the dog on and it  gazed up at her she just lit up her  behavior changed 180 degrees  [Music]  japanese are primed for robotic  companionship growing up with friendly  cartoon androids like daryaman  but one robot startup is proving looks  aren't everything despite having neither  head arms nor legs the kubo bots sold  more than 30 000 units by september  many to stressed out users working from  home under covet restrictions the retail  price starts at about two hundred  dollars  yukai engineering ceo shinsuke aoki said  kubo leverages the most pleasing parts  of a pet a fluffy torso and a wagging  tail no idea at first it seemed weird he  said but when you pet an animal like a  cat you usually don't bother to look at  its face  but frazzled adults aren't the only  japanese turning to machine  companionship at this kindergarten in  central japan robots have replaced the  class guinea pig or bunny teachers say  the robots not only reduce stress  they're teaching kids to be more humane  two years ago moriyama kindergarten  bought a roly-poly pair of bots named  rice cake and coco weighing as much as  an infant with a price tag of a french  bulldog the cybernetic machines are  designed to love bomb their owners or in  this case a room full of fidgety  five-year-olds  our kids think the robots are alive said  principal kyoshin kodama the bots have  encouraged the kids to take better care  of things be kinder to each other and  cooperate more  love ott is a so-called emotional robot  programmed to autonomously navigate its  surroundings and respond to its owners  over the last year sales have  skyrocketed  robots are usually hard cold and inhuman  said gruvx company spokesperson miki  ikegami but since our bots are built to  soothe we made them warm and soft  japan's oldest and most successful  social robot is an fda approved device  called pado resembling an ordinary plush  toy the artificial intelligence powered  bot customizes its response as it gets  to know each patient they conducted  randomized control treatment inventor  takanori shibata said clinical trials  have validated the device's benefits as  a non-drug therapy interaction with  parallel can improve  depression  anxiety pain and also they improve the  mood of the people  since launching in 1998 thousands of  pato robots have gone into service  worldwide relieving stress among  children in icus  treating veterans suffering from ptsd  and helping dementia patients like real  pets potto has been shown to stimulate  brain activity helping reconnect damaged  areas one day they didn't speak for more  than 10 years but when she started to  talk to paolo she recovered her speech  neuroscientist julie robiard of the  university of british columbia said much  remains unknown about how well social  robots really work what we can say from  the science right now is that robots  have a huge amount of potential  and discovering that potential is all  the more urgent now in the covet era as  robots offer the promise of social  connection without social contact  they can be an emotional support they  could be a calming presence in the home  but they can also help us in other ways  they can demonstrate rehab exercises  they can distract a child who's going  through a painful procedure or a  vaccination  creators say intelligent social robots  will never replace humans but when  companions caregivers or therapists  aren't available robots are lending a  friendly paw and are already earning  their keep  for cbs mornings lucy craft tokyo  yep i'm all for it whatever works for  you i mean yeah it wouldn't work for me  yeah me neither but if we all grew up  loving r2d2 and johnny five from short  circuit now that it's reality you don't  want to robot dog tony no  no no you want the real thing i will say  this if i ever need to have my brain  activity stimulated and i hope that's no  time soon i would like one and i i see  the value for little children too that's  true but nothing can replace just the  feel and love the unconditional love  don't let me have a dog so maybe this is  the best option for i i won't let my  wife have a dog maybe we should have  slopped
 

